# Supabase Postgres connection string. Copy this file to api/.env and replace
# [YOUR-PROJECT-REF], [YOUR-REGION] and [YOUR-PASSWORD] with the values from the
# Supabase dashboard, percent-encoding any special characters in the password.
#
# This is the Supavisor pooler endpoint, not the direct one. The direct host
# (db.<ref>.supabase.co) resolves to an IPv6 address only, so it is unreachable
# from an IPv4-only network unless the project buys the IPv4 add-on. The pooler
# has an A record, so it works either way. Note the username carries the project
# ref (postgres.<ref>) — that is how the pooler picks the tenant — and the host
# names the project's region.
#
# Port 6543 is transaction mode: a backend is handed back to the pool after each
# transaction, so connections are cheap but no session state survives between
# statements. pgx defaults to caching prepared statements under generated names,
# which breaks here — the second query to reuse a backend fails with
# "prepared statement ... already exists" (SQLSTATE 42P05). Hence
# default_query_exec_mode=exec, which keeps the extended protocol (so parameters
# are still bound server-side, not interpolated) but stops naming and caching
# statements. Port 5432 is session mode, where the cache works and this
# parameter should be dropped.
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://postgres.[YOUR-PROJECT-REF]:[YOUR-PASSWORD]@aws-0-[YOUR-REGION].pooler.supabase.com:6543/postgres?default_query_exec_mode=exec

# After pointing at a database, `go run ./cmd/pgcheck` verifies the connection:
# it prints the server version and what the app's tables hold, and writes
# nothing.
